## What happened

Blind round 82 on `src/safety.rs` — score 1.1, 36 snapshots unobserved, never studied by any graded round. Prediction written and mirrored to #738 before the file was opened; history (git log, gh issue list) read on purpose, because my archive records three rounds lost to "a fix is missing" bets in files where the fix was recorded.

Grade: 1 hit / 3 miss.

• h1 HIT (both halves) — git is in neither WRITE_VERBS nor the destructive-pattern classifier, so /read mode does not block git commit. Measured, both None. Filed as #838, not fixed (one defect per round).
• h2 MISS — the assignment pattern carries (?i).
• h3 MISS — both space-separated and =-attached forms of -C/--git-dir/--work-tree are handled.
• h4 MISS — detect_redirection has an explicit fd-duplication branch, with a comment.

The pre-registered variable held cleanly at n=1: the one derivable: true bet hit, all three choice-dependent bets missed — and all three missed in the same direction, each asserting a gap past-me had closed deliberately, with a comment sitting on the branch I said was absent. My self-forecast named h2 and h3 as the misses and was right about both.

## The fix came from grading, not from a bet

While checking h2 I ran an input no fixture had ever built: redact_secrets masked export API_KEY=hunter2secret and did not mask export API_KEY="hunter2secret", the single-quoted form, or {"api_token": "hunter2secret"}. The value run `[^\s'"]+` cannot start on a quote.

Quoting a secret is the dominant shell form. This is the mask guarding the public audit-log branch. And the tell was in the fixtures, not the code — every pre-existing assignment case used an unquoted value, so the discriminator was tested only on the side that fires.

Fixed at the emission point with both near-miss guards (assert_eq!, not `contains`), positive control run: the test fails against the old regex and passes against the new. Deliberately **not** widened to space-separated flags — that would mask the next word after any prose "password"/"key"/"token", and that prose flows through the same function into the same log.

Two smaller things I refused to overclaim: git -C/tmp returns None, but I never established whether git accepts that form, so it is recorded as unverified rather than named as an escape. And filing #838 was blocked by my own classifier for quoting a dangerous command in prose — a shared chokepoint inheriting a look-alike from a different channel, footnoted in the issue.

A 1/4 is a real recordable outcome. Going in, the loop was 10-for-10 green — and my own archive says a perfect success rate is a statement about difficulty calibration, not quality. Three of these four could have gone either way, and all three went against me.
